Build succeeds. Here's what I built.

**Structure** (the order a collector actually reads in):
1. **Masthead** — sticky, hairline-ruled: house name, caliber, section nav, a quiet "Reserve" link.
2. **Hero** — "Caliber 08" at display scale, the run-of-200 line as lede, the 72-hour sentence as body, two actions, then a five-cell figures strip (72 h · 2.5 Hz · 214 · 62 h · 200) that turns the abstract claim into numbers before any scrolling.
3. **01 — How the energy travels** — the six stages as a numbered chain, in the given order, each with a dot on the rule, its detail at reading measure, and its figure pulled into a right-hand monospace column so the numbers form their own scannable spine.
4. **02 — Four layers, front to back** — the four layers with thickness set in mono and a bar drawn to relative scale (against the 1.4mm main plate), so the stack reads as a physical object.
5. **03 — Specification** — all ten rows, two columns, dot leaders, values in mono.
6. **04 — Three finishes** — three cards with exact CHF price, delivery date, remaining allocation, plus a gauge of remaining-against-200. "Choose this finish" selects it and scrolls to the form; the card then shows as selected.
7. **05 — The workshop** — the five facts as numbered editorial notes in serif, including the run of 200 and the indefinite servicing commitment.
8. **06 — Reserve** — the form (select/name/email, all labels intact) with a live summary line of the chosen finish, price, and date. The non-binding/no-payment statement sits beside it, before submitting. Confirmation names the finish, price, email and delivery date, and repeats that nothing is binding.
9. **Footer** — enquiries mailto, the three link labels, copyright.

**Look**: near-black ground with a faint warm bloom behind the title, warm off-white text, brass accent used only for figures and the selected state, steel-cool nothing else. Old-style serif for headings and workshop prose, mono for every number (the page's numbers are the product), sans for UI. Hairline rules instead of boxes; one 14px fade-up on scroll, disabled under `prefers-reduced-motion`.

All required content is preserved verbatim, including stage order, layer order, and every spec row. Nothing committed.